"1. Load the full MovieLens100k `u.data` file using the correct delimiter (`\\t`) and four columns in order: `user_id`, `item_id`, `rating`, `timestamp`.\n2. Validate successful parsing by enforcing numeric types for all four columns and handling malformed rows explicitly (fail with a clear error or report dropped rows).\n3. Compute the exact count of unique `user_id` values across the full file.\n4. Compute the exact count of unique `item_id` values across the full file.\n5. Count ratings per `user_id` over the entire dataset and rank users by descending rating count.\n6. Report the Top 3 most active users, including each user_id and their total number of ratings.\n7. For the single most active user (#1 by rating count), compute and report their mean `rating` across all of their ratings.\n8. Filter ratings for `item_id` 50 and `item_id` 181 and compute co-occurrence as the number of distinct users who rated both items.\n9. Use set intersection on distinct user_id sets for items 50 and 181 to avoid double-counting duplicate ratings if present.\n10. Assess timestamp validity against the expected MovieLens 100k 1997\u20131998 period by converting Unix timestamps to human-readable dates.\n11. Define and document the expected timestamp range from the dataset context, then identify and report any timestamps earlier or later than that range.\n12. Report the minimum and maximum timestamp found in the file, with corresponding converted datetimes, to support the range check.\n13. Do not infer or hardcode expected counts or results; compute all outputs directly from the provided file.\n14. Ensure the script runs end-to-end without manual intervention and prints clearly labeled results for all requested analyses."